<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Kition</h1><p class="st-meta">850 BCE – 312 BCE (approx.) · capital Kition</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Phoenician kingdom on Cyprus, founded by Tyre, that conquered its Greek neighbours Idalion and Tamassos and was the birthplace of Zeno the Stoic.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kition, at modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larnaca">Larnaca</a>, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-working town. In the 9th century BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tyre">Tyre</a> settled there and built a great temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Astarte</a> on the ruins of an earlier sanctuary. Kition probably began as a Tyrian dependency, and in 707 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> set up a stele there recording Cypriot submission.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>From the early 5th century BCE an independent Phoenician dynasty ruled Kition, beginning with Baalmelek I, and it was loyal to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>. Kition conquered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-idalion">Idalion</a> c. 450 BCE, its kings then styling themselves rulers of both, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pumiyaton">Pumiyaton</a> later bought <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-tamassos">Tamassos</a>. Its harbour served Persian fleets, and its kings issued coins showing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melqart">Heracles-Melqart</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I</a> executed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pumiyaton">Pumiyaton</a> and annexed the kingdom in 312 BCE. Kition continued as a Ptolemaic and Roman city; its most famous son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeno-of-citium">Zeno</a>, founded Stoic philosophy at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 850 BCE – 312 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:42.79%;width:10.71%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>850 BCE – 312 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kition</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Baalmelek I, Azbaal, Pumiyaton</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Phoenician religion; cults of Astarte and Melqart</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Phoenician</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Cyprus</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kition, Citium</dd></dl></aside></div>
